\description{
Tests whether samples are more similar to the other samples in their group
than to samples in other groups. Gives the same statistics as
\code{vegan::mrpp()}: the observed and expected weighted mean within-group
distance (\eqn{\delta}), the chance-corrected within-group agreement
\eqn{A}, the mean distance within each group, and a permutation p-value.
Also gives the standardized effect size \eqn{z} that
\code{summary(vegan::permustats())} reports. Computes them in parallel C,
straight from the distances.
}
\details{
\strong{Method}

MRPP (Mielke et al. 1976; Mielke & Berry 2001) asks whether samples in the
same group are closer to each other than samples drawn at random. With
\eqn{\bar{d}_g} the mean distance between the \eqn{n_g (n_g - 1) / 2}
pairs of samples in group \eqn{g}, the test statistic is their weighted
mean
\deqn{\delta = \sum_g C_g \bar{d}_g, \quad C_g = w_g / \sum_h w_h}
with weights \eqn{w_g} from \code{weights}. Groups of one sample have no pairs,
so they are left out, as in \code{vegan::mrpp()}. The expected value of
\eqn{\delta} over all permutations of the samples is the mean of all the
distances, and the chance-corrected within-group agreement is
\eqn{A = 1 - \delta / E(\delta)} (McCune & Grace 2002): 1 when samples are
identical within groups, 0 when groups are no more alike than chance, and
negative when they are less alike.

For each permutation, the group labels are shuffled among the samples and
\eqn{\delta} is recomputed. The p-value is
\eqn{(1 + \#\{\delta_{perm} \le \delta\}) / (1 + permutations)}. As in
\code{vegan::mrpp()}, permuted values within \code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps)} of the
observed one count as ties. The standardized effect size \eqn{z} compares
the observed \eqn{\delta} with the permuted ones, using their mean and
(sample) standard deviation, as \code{summary(vegan::permustats())} does. It is
\code{NA} with fewer than two permutations, or when every permutation gives the
same \eqn{\delta}.

\strong{Speed and memory}

With \eqn{S_g} the sum of the distances within group \eqn{g},
\eqn{\delta = \sum_g c_g S_g} for constants \eqn{c_g}. So each
permutation costs one comparison per pair of samples, read straight from
\code{dm}, with no \eqn{n \times n} matrix. Many permutations share each pass
over the distances, and permutations run in parallel.

\strong{Reproducibility}

The permutations come from \code{seed}, not from R's random number generator,
so \code{set.seed()} has no effect, and each permutation has a random number
stream of its own. Results are identical from run to run and for any
number of \code{cpus}. The random permutations differ from \code{vegan::mrpp()}'s,
so p-values and \eqn{z} agree with it only up to sampling error, unless
the same \code{permutations} matrix is given to both.

\strong{Differences from vegan::mrpp()}
\itemize{
\item \code{mrpp()} takes distances, not a community table: compute them first
with \code{\link[=beta_div]{beta_div()}} or a metric function such as \code{\link[=bray]{bray()}}.
\item \code{A} is reported even with \code{permutations = 0}; \code{vegan::mrpp()} gives
\code{NA} then.
\item A group of one sample has a \code{class_delta} of \code{NA}, where
\code{vegan::mrpp()} has \code{NaN}.
\item \code{mrpp()} does not compute the classification strength (\code{CS}), which
\code{vegan::mrpp()} also leaves \code{NA}.
\item \code{strata} permutes freely within strata. For other restricted designs,
pass a \code{permutations} matrix from \code{permute::shuffleSet()}.
}
}
\section{Pairwise comparisons}{

With \code{pairwise = TRUE}, each pair of groups is also tested on its own,
as q2-diversity's \code{beta_group_significance(pairwise=True)} does: the
distances are cut down to the two groups' samples, and the same test is
run on them, with the same number of \code{permutations}, the same \code{strata}
(cut down too), and the test's other arguments. The overall test is
unchanged.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Permutations:} Each pair has random permutations of its own, drawn
from \code{seed} and the pair's position in the table, never from the
number of \code{cpus}. So a pair's p-value is reproducible, but differs
(by sampling error) from that of the same test run by hand on the
pair's samples with the same \code{seed}. A supplied \code{permutations} matrix
is cut down to each pair's samples by following each permutation's
cycles: each of the pair's samples goes to the next of the pair's
samples on its cycle. That gives a permutation of the pair's samples,
uniformly distributed when the full one is, which keeps any
restriction the matrix has, such as permuting only within strata.
\item \strong{Multiple testing:} The pairs' permutation p-values are adjusted
together with \code{stats::p.adjust(method = p_adjust)}. The default,
Holm's method, controls the family-wise error rate whatever the
dependence between the pairs' tests. \code{'BH'} controls the false
discovery rate instead, as q2-diversity's q-values do. The raw
p-values are kept as well.
\item \strong{Pairs that can't be tested:} A pair the test can't be run on, such
as two groups of one sample each, gets \code{NA}s, with a warning, rather
than an error. A test's other warnings for a pair, such as all of its
distances being 0, are passed on, naming the pair.
}

The \code{pairwise} field of the result is a data frame with one row per pair
of groups, named like \code{"a-b"}, in the order of the groups' factor levels
(sorted values, for other vectors). Its columns are the test's
statistics for the pair, \code{z}, \code{n} (the pair's number of samples),
\code{n_perm}, \code{p_value} (unadjusted) and \code{p_adjusted}. The \code{p_adjust} field
names the method, and \code{print()} shows both.
}

\examples{
    # 30 samples in three treatment groups. The 'high' group has more
    # of the first five genera.
    set.seed(1)
    counts <- matrix(rpois(30 * 20, 5), 30, 20)
    rownames(counts) <- paste0('S', 1:30)
    meta <- data.frame(
      row.names = rownames(counts),
      treatment = rep(c('ctrl', 'low', 'high'), each = 10),
      site      = rep(c('A', 'B'), 15) )
    high <- meta$treatment == 'high'
    counts[high, 1:5] <- counts[high, 1:5] + 5

    dm <- bray(counts)

    # Are samples more alike within treatment groups?
    res <- mrpp(dm, 'treatment', meta)
    res

    # Each group's mean within-group distance, and the permutations.
    plot(res)
    plot(res, type = 'null')

    # The same, with the groups as a vector.
    res <- mrpp(dm, meta$treatment)
    as.data.frame(res)

    # Permuting only within sites.
    mrpp(dm, 'treatment', meta, strata = 'site')

    # Each pair of treatments, with Holm-adjusted p-values.
    mrpp(dm, 'treatment', meta, pairwise = TRUE)$pairwise

}
